  "question": {
    "text": "$F(x)=\\cos x-1+\\frac{x^2}{2!}, x \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Then $f(x)$ is",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "O2gBX",
      "text": "decreasing function",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "ZSG1C",
      "text": "increasing function",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "Z9h8f",
      "text": "neither increasing nor decreasing",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "Nr62q",
      "text": "constant $\\forall x\\gt0$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Hint : $f(x)=\\cos x-1+\\frac{x^2}{2!}, x \\in R \\Rightarrow f^{\\prime}(x)=-\\sin x-0+\\frac{2 x}{2!}=x-\\sin x$\n<img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/wbjee/U6EjYIlRmpk58Suygcjysz9-CxcE7FTBuPSxDbMrp7E.original.fullsize.png\"/>\n$\\begin{array}{lll}\nf^{\\prime}(x)\\gt0 & \\forall x\\gt0 & \\because x\\gt\\sin x \\\\\nf^{\\prime}(x) \\lt 0 & \\forall x \\lt 0 & \\because x \\lt \\sin x\n\\end{array}$\n$\\therefore \\quad f(x)$ is neither increasing nor decreasing .",
    "image": null
  },
